So far as I can tell (and/or recall) there are three practices, possibly di=
ctated in part by texts being translated or those most familiar to the auth=
or: full quotes as object of a verb of speaking, full quotes with a parenth=
etical verb of speaking, no quotes and separate lines -- and often no verbs=
 of speaking after a conversation gets started, at least with with only two=
 interlocutors. I assume that in all cases the purported isomorphism is wit=
h the speakers not the speech.




